WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.125 --> 00:00:10.465
A respiratory therapist starts gathering
clues before a patient even speaks.

2
00:00:10.958 --> 00:00:13.638
Breathing effort, chest movement, posture,

3
00:00:13.838 --> 00:00:15.678
and skin color provide vital information.

4
00:00:16.374 --> 00:00:20.214
This immediate process is called physical
assessment when entering the room.

5
00:00:20.833 --> 00:00:22.906
Welcome to Lesson 1.2

6
00:00:23.125 --> 00:00:25.365
By the end, you'll describe bedside
assessment,

7
00:00:26.144 --> 00:00:27.864
recognize findings, and connect these to
diseases.

8
00:00:28.958 --> 00:00:32.558
You will understand wheezes, crackles,
dullness,

9
00:00:33.198 --> 00:00:34.958
hyperresonance, and tracheal shifts.

10
00:00:37.208 --> 00:00:39.768
Physical assessment follows a four-step
sequence.

11
00:00:40.208 --> 00:00:43.508
First, inspect breathing effort, posture,

12
00:00:43.748 --> 00:00:45.468
chest symmetry, and skin color.

13
00:00:45.958 --> 00:00:49.438
Accessory muscle use or bluish skin may
show respiratory distress.

14
00:00:53.458 --> 00:00:56.118
Next, palpate the chest to assess
expansion,

15
00:00:56.618 --> 00:00:58.478
tracheal position, and tactile fremitus.

16
00:00:58.958 --> 00:01:02.318
Tactile fremitus are vibrations felt when
the patient speaks.

17
00:01:02.833 --> 00:01:06.333
These clues help identify abnormalities
beneath the skin.

18
00:01:10.333 --> 00:01:14.253
Then, percuss the chest wall by tapping to
estimate underlying

19
00:01:14.253 --> 00:01:14.693
structures.

20
00:01:15.291 --> 00:01:17.251
Normal lungs sound resonant.

21
00:01:17.416 --> 00:01:21.456
Fluid or dense tissue sounds dull, and
trapped air sounds

22
00:01:21.456 --> 00:01:22.276
hyper-resonant.

23
00:01:26.125 --> 00:01:29.605
Finally, auscultate with a stethoscope to
listen for breath sounds.

24
00:01:30.958 --> 00:01:34.298
Compare both sides of the chest for normal
and abnormal sounds.

25
00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:36.789
This completes the physical assessment
sequence.

26
00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:43.833
Single findings rarely tell the whole
story.

27
00:01:44.166 --> 00:01:48.026
Clusters of findings suggest patterns
important for diagnosis.

28
00:01:48.791 --> 00:01:53.691
For example, hyperresonance with absent
breath sounds may indicate trapped

29
00:01:53.691 --> 00:01:54.571
air under pressure.

30
00:01:58.875 --> 00:02:04.695
Dull percussion combined with increased
vibration and bronchial breath sounds may suggest lung consolidation.

31
00:02:05.208 --> 00:02:08.248
Recognizing these patterns is key to
clinical reasoning.

32
00:02:08.708 --> 00:02:12.348
The goal is to describe findings and
communicate concerns clearly.

33
00:02:16.333 --> 00:02:18.273
Watch for important red flags.

34
00:02:18.666 --> 00:02:23.626
Tracheal deviation combined with absent
breath sounds or hyperresonance is urgent.

35
00:02:23.958 --> 00:02:28.538
Immediate reporting is essential when
respiratory distress worsens with one-sided,

36
00:02:28.998 --> 00:02:29.578
absent breath sounds.

37
00:02:30.166 --> 00:02:31.506
Remember the sequence.

38
00:02:32.366 --> 00:02:33.806
Inspect, palpate, percuss,

39
00:02:34.466 --> 00:02:34.906
auscultate.

40
00:02:35.416 --> 00:02:40.136
This sequence reveals critical information
and forms the foundation of respiratory care reasoning.

41
00:02:40.541 --> 00:02:42.841
Let's begin your journey into physical
assessment.

